  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Plot the graph of $x=-{y}^{2}+\\frac{7y}{2}.$",
  "worked_solution": "To plot $x=-{y}^{2}+\\frac{7y}{2},$ we will first plot the function $y=-{x}^{2}+\\frac{7x}{2}$ and then reflect it over the line $y=x.$\n\nThe curve $y=-{x}^{2}+\\frac{7x}{2}$ is a parabola. It has the following factored form:\n\n$$\ny=-x(x-\\frac{7}{2})\n$$\n\nFrom the factored form above, we get the following graph:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14240562/assets/ab305b89891508770d709b4913fef0f06360ed6aed05cdd32de6f41eba527207.png)\n\nNow, to get the curve $x=-{y}^{2}+\\frac{7y}{2},$ we just have to reflect the curve $y=-{x}^{2}+\\frac{7x}{2}$ over the line $y=x,$ as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14240562/assets/63c6861e06dfaf818e6b6a30f2ca995c69dd6a7bdecc1b305c5c2b098712a64f.png)",
